error[E0201]: chars() builds a list[u32; M], but list[i64; 4] is expected
 --> tests/errors/E0201_utf8.al:5:31
  |
5 |     let wrong: list[i64; 4] = chars(s);
  |                               ^^^^^^^^
  = hint: store it in a variable of that type: let cs: list[u32; 64] = chars(s);

error[E0216]: chars() takes its type, and so its capacity, from the variable it is stored in
 --> tests/errors/E0201_utf8.al:6:11
  |
6 |     print(chars(s));
  |           ^^^^^^^^
  = hint: make the call the whole right-hand side of a typed let/var, an assignment or a return: let cs: list[u32; 64] = chars(s); or, to go through the code points one at a time without a list: for c in chars(s) { }

error[E0201]: push_char() needs a code point, an integer, found str[1]
 --> tests/errors/E0201_utf8.al:7:22
  |
7 |     push_char(mut s, "b");
  |                      ^^^
  = hint: to add text, use append(mut s, "b")

error[E0207]: push_char() modifies its first argument
 --> tests/errors/E0201_utf8.al:8:15
  |
8 |     push_char(s, 98);
  |               ^
  = hint: write push_char(mut s, c)

